{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The lotteries have distinct monetary outcomes: Lottery A has outcomes of 2500, 2400, and 0 Euros with probabilities 33%, 66%, and 1%, respectively. Lottery B has a sure outcome of 2400 Euros with 100% probability. The reason identifies the minimum absolute value outcome as the 'sure outcome'—here, 0 Euros is the smallest absolute value. The differences between this sure outcome (0) and the other outcomes (2500 and 2400) are 2500 and 2400, respectively. These differences are then weighted by their respective probabilities in Lottery A (33% and 66%), and the sum of the sure outcome (0) and these weighted differences is computed. For Lottery B, since all outcomes are 2400, the sure outcome is 2400, and the differences from 0 are 2400 (with probability 1), so the weighted sum is 2400. The reason compares the resulting sums for both lotteries to determine preference.",
